
########## frequency.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

        7  [P] from the preprint
       13  [V] verified against a named source
        9  [A] assumed range, swept

1. The band, and the lattice it is said to drive

  [P] F_micro                                 1,000 -    1,000,000 Hz

  [V] Raman-active modes measured in a layered oxide cathode (NMC111):
        wavenumber      frequency      quantum
            144 cm-1        4.32 THz     17.85 meV
            189 cm-1        5.67 THz     23.43 meV
            324 cm-1        9.71 THz     40.17 meV

      the lowest measured mode is               4.32 THz  = 4,317,011 MHz
      the top of the F_micro band is               1 MHz
      ratio at the top of the band         4,317,011 x   (6.6 orders)
      ratio at the bottom of the band   4,317,011,395 x   (9.6 orders)

      So the band sits between six and ten orders of magnitude below the
      slowest lattice vibration measured in this class of material.
      'Phonon-assisted tunneling' at 1 MHz is not addressing a phonon;
      there is no mode there to assist.

2. The same point in energy, which is the form that matters for tunneling

      h.f at 1 kHz                         4.136e-12 eV
      h.f at 1 MHz                         4.136e-09 eV
      k.T at 300 K                           0.02585 eV
        ratio, kT / h.f(1 MHz)             6,250,986 x

  [V] sodium migration barrier                  0.10 - 0.64 eV
        E_a / h.f(1 MHz) at 0.10 eV         2.418e+07
        E_a / h.f(1 MHz) at 0.64 eV         1.548e+08

      The stimulation quantum is about six million times smaller than the
      ambient thermal energy the ions already have, and seven to eight
      orders below the barrier it is meant to lower.  Whatever a kHz-to-MHz
      field does to a cell, it does not do it one quantum at a time.

3. Where the cell has a faradaic response at all

  [V] charge-transfer semicircle                   1 - 1,000 Hz
  [P] F_micro band                             1,000 - 1,000,000 Hz
      overlap                                      0 Hz

      The two bands meet at a point and do not overlap.  Above the
      charge-transfer region the measured response of a cell is ohmic and
      inductive, which means current in the F_micro band flows through the
      double-layer capacitance and the series inductance rather than across
      the interface.  Capacitive current transfers no ions; it dissipates in
      the series resistance.  That is Joule heating, which is the loss Sec. 1
      sets out to avoid.

4. The limitation Sec. 5 names, and the one it should have named

  [P] Sec. 5: "MHz-range fractal signals injected into such geometries will
      suffer from severe attenuation and skin-effect reflections."

         frequency   skin depth, Cu   skin depth, Al
          1,000 Hz        2062.9 um        2590.9 um
         10,000 Hz         652.3 um         819.3 um
        100,000 Hz         206.3 um         259.1 um
      1,000,000 Hz          65.2 um          81.9 um
      10,000,000 Hz          20.6 um          25.9 um

      [A] current-collector foil is 6-20 um thick
      at 1 MHz the aluminium skin depth is 82 um, i.e. 4.1-13.7x
      the foil thickness, so the current still fills the foil and skin
      effect within it is not the binding limit at the stated frequencies.

      Sec. 5 reaches the right conclusion -- that MHz signals do not reach
      the lattice in a wound cell -- from the wrong mechanism.  The binding
      limit is the double-layer shunt measured in Sec. 3 above, and it
      applies to a planar coin cell too, which is the geometry Sec. 5
      prescribes as the fix.

########## anomaly.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

        7  [P] from the preprint
       13  [V] verified against a named source
        9  [A] assumed range, swept

1. The heading and the body describe different quantities

      heading:  Negative Differential Resistance
      body:     Z_diff = dV/dI, and an anomaly when dT per unit power falls

      Negative differential resistance means dV/dI < 0.  For a cell under
      charge at fixed state of charge, V = OCV(SoC) + I.R_i with R_i > 0,
      so dV/dI = R_i > 0 always.  A cell cannot exhibit NDR in that sense;
      the terminal voltage rises with current by construction.

      'Sub-proportional heat per unit power delivered' is a statement about
      energy efficiency -- more of the input ended up stored rather than
      dissipated.  That is a real and measurable thing, and it is not a sign
      change in dV/dI.  The two are not the same criterion, and the section
      gives no way to decide which one the optimiser is to search for.

      Also, dV/dI has units of ohms, so it is a differential RESISTANCE.
      The text calls it a differential impedance, which is the complex
      quantity measured against frequency, not against current.

2. Can the body's criterion be told apart from the cell warming up?

  [P] Sec. 5 identifies this confound itself:
      "Differentiating between standard thermal agitation (which naturally
       lowers R_i as the cell heats up) and genuine electro-kinetic field
       enhancement requires highly precise isothermal calorimetry"

      Arrhenius, R_i(T) = R0.exp(E_a/kT), relative to 25 C:
      [A]    E_a      25 C      35 C      45 C      55 C
        0.20     1.000     0.777     0.613     0.491
        0.50     1.000     0.532     0.294     0.169

      A 20 K rise alone multiplies I^2.R heating at fixed current by
      0.29 to 0.61, i.e. it cuts it by 1.6 to 3.4 times.

      That is exactly the signature Sec. 2.2 looks for: push more power in,
      get less heat out per unit of it.  So the criterion as written is
      satisfied by a cell that is merely warming, across the whole swept
      range of activation energies, with no electro-kinetic effect at all.

      Sec. 5 prescribes the right instrument -- isothermal calorimetry --
      and that is to the paper's credit.  But Sec. 2.2 is the section that
      defines the search objective, and as defined it is confounded.  The
      optimiser would find warm cells.

3. What the paper's own pulsed-charging citation says instead

  [V] Purushothaman & Landau, J. Electrochem. Soc. 153(3) A533 (2006),
      the preprint's only pulsed-charging reference:

      "Modeling the lithium diffusion into a homogeneous intercalant layer
       indicates that the lithium concentration reaches saturation at the
       graphite/electrolyte interface after about 1 h under conventional
       constant current charging"

      the analysed protocol: 3 s on, 1 s off
        duty cycle                                 75%
        repetition rate                           0.25 Hz

      "A nonlinearly decreasing current density profile which conforms to
       the mass transfer coefficient variation was shown to provide complete
       charging in less than 3/4 of an hour, faster than any other
       pulse-charging profile studied."
        best result is a continuous profile:  True

      So the limiting mechanism in the cited work is solid-state diffusion,
      the beneficial timescale is a quarter of a hertz, and the best result
      is not a pulse at all.  It supports W_macro, the low-frequency
      envelope, and contradicts F_micro, which sits six orders of magnitude
      above the band it identifies.  The paper cites no source that
      identifies a beneficial kHz or MHz band, and the audit of all six of
      its references found none -- see the reference-audit repository.

########## throughput.py ##########

0. Where the constants in this script come from

        7  [P] from the preprint
       13  [V] verified against a named source
        9  [A] assumed range, swept

1. Evaluations per day

  [P] cells, one per GPU                                  8

      [A]   C-rate   charge+discharge      per day
              0.5C             4.0 h           48
              1.0C             2.0 h           96
              2.0C             1.0 h          192

      One evaluation of a candidate waveform is at minimum one charge, and
      a comparable discharge to return the cell to a known state.  Nothing
      about the GPU changes that number.

2. The ceiling, which arrives before the search finishes

      [A] coin-cell useful life           500 - 3,000 cycles
      evaluations available from 8 cells, ever:
             500 cycles ->    4,000 evaluations,     42 days of continuous 1C cycling
           3,000 cycles ->   24,000 evaluations,    250 days of continuous 1C cycling

      [A] particle swarm optimisation typically needs 10,000 - 100,000
          evaluations on a 10-30 dimensional space, and Sec. 3 calls its own
          parameter space 'hyperdimensional'.

      So the whole lifetime budget of one set of samples, 24,000
      evaluations, is at the bottom of the range a PSO run needs, and the
      sample set is destroyed in reaching it.

3. The confound this creates, which the preprint does not mention

      The objective function is the cell's internal resistance, and internal
      resistance rises with cycle count.  So the quantity being minimised
      drifts upward over exactly the timescale of the search, in a direction
      that depends on how many evaluations each cell has already served.

      A swarm that shares its best parameter vector across nodes -- which is
      what Sec. 3 proposes -- shares it between cells of different age.  Any
      apparent improvement found early is confounded with the samples being
      newer, and Sec. 5's isothermal calorimetry does not address this one.

      Sec. 3's own answer, a 'Dynamic Key Trajectory parameterized by the
      State of Charge', addresses drift within a cycle.  Ageing is drift
      across cycles and is not parameterised by SoC.

4. What the GPUs are actually for

      [A] DDS stream per cell:
              10 MSa/s x 16 bit =      20 MB/s   ->      160 MB/s for 8
             100 MSa/s x 16 bit =     200 MB/s   ->     1600 MB/s for 8

      That is a data-movement task. It needs bandwidth and determinism, not
      floating-point throughput, and nothing in Sec. 3 describes a
      computation that would occupy a GPU between evaluations that arrive
      every two hours.

      Sec. 4 then says the opposite of Sec. 3 about where generation
      happens:
  [P] "Microsecond-level current/voltage logging fed into a high-speed DSP
      or FPGA (e.g. STM32/Zynq architectures) acting as the local generative
      engine."

      If the local FPGA is the generative engine then the GPU is not
      streaming the waveform, and the 1:1 GPU-to-cell mapping has no work
      left to do. The two sections describe two different machines.

5. Sec. 6 against what the deposit contains

  [P] Sec. 6, "Structure of Provided Data (Zenodo Repository)":
          /Hardware: Gerber, BOM, 3D STEP
          /Software: CUDA kernels, PSO synchronization scripts, DDS streaming code
          /Datasets: high-speed DAQ logs, isothermal calorimetry, voltage curves

      the deposit holds                      1 file, 97,786 bytes
      which is the four-page PDF and nothing else.

  [P] the PDF header proposes:   CC BY 4.0 for text and data, CERN-OHL-S for hardware
      the Zenodo record carries: cc-by-4.0 only

      The section is written in the present tense about data that is not
      there. That is a stronger version of the same pattern as the cymatic
      deposit's Sec. 6, which promises a GitHub repository in the future
      tense and names none.
